 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of rays per batch (1024) and the number of samples per ray (128 total: 64 coarse + 64 fine) [S1]. NerfAcc and Instant-NGP offer fast convergence [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the feature volume (for Control-NeRF) or the network weights. Hardware: Single RTX A6000 GPU and 128 GB RAM [S2].
-*   **Performance:** PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS metrics are reported for Control-NeRF on LLFF datasets (e.g., Fern, T-Rex) [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Fast convergence with NerfAcc/Instant-NGP [S2]; ability to manipulate scenes via feature volumes (Control-NeRF) [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Restricted to static scenes with minimal moving objects (surgical applications) [S2]; requires careful ray sampling to avoid artifacts [S2].
-*   **When to Use:** Static scene rendering, surgical video analysis, or when editable feature volumes are needed [S1, S2].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Dynamic scenes with significant motion, or when real-time performance on low-end hardware is required without acceleration techniques [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The two-stage sampling (coarse + fine) increases the number of network queries per ray compared to single-stage methods, but the use of Instant-NGP and NerfAcc accelerates convergence [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the 64-dimensional feature volume $V_s$ in memory [S1]. The hardware setup uses 128 GB RAM, which is sufficient for typical surgical scene volumes [S2].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Trained on a single RTX A6000 GPU [S2]. Specific PSNR/SSIM metrics are provided for Control-NeRF on LLFF datasets (e.g., Fern PSNR 25.752, T-Rex PSNR 26.510) [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Fast convergence on static scenes; feature-volume approach allows for scene manipulation and editing [S1, S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Restricted to static scenes with minimal dynamic elements (surgical tools, moving organs) [S2].
+*   **When to Use:** When fast training on static scenes is required and scene manipulation is desired [S1, S2].
+*   **When Not to Use:** For highly dynamic scenes or when explicit 3D position encoding is preferred for geometry [S1, S2].
 
 ## Background
-The NeRF Training Setup described here originates from the Control-NeRF paper, which introduced editable feature volumes for scene rendering and manipulation [S1]. It was applied to laparoscopic surgical videos to render static abdominal environments, leveraging the Nerfstudio library for efficiency [S2]. The Image-Based NeRF variant is based on standard practices in differentiable rendering, often involving Eikonal loss for geometry regularization [S3, unofficial].
+The method originates from the Control-NeRF paper, which introduced editable feature volumes for scene rendering and manipulation [S1]. It was adapted for laparoscopic surgical video rendering using the Nerfstudio library, NerfAcc, and Instant-NGP to handle static abdominal environments [S2]. The approach modifies the standard NeRF architecture by replacing positional encoding of 3D positions with sampled feature vectors [S1].
 
